WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.742 --> 00:00:12.200
PRAJAKTA DAMLE:
Welcome, everybody,

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.430
and thank you for
being here today.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.810
My name is Prajakta Damle,
and I lead product management

00:00:19.810 --> 00:00:22.240
for Dataplex at Google Cloud.

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:25.030
And joining me
today is John Allen,

00:00:25.030 --> 00:00:27.400
head of data and
analytics product

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.110
strategy at Deutsche Bank.

00:00:30.110 --> 00:00:33.470
John has been with Deutsche
Bank for over 9 years,

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:37.310
building their data platforms,
and enabling and championing

00:00:37.310 --> 00:00:41.400
big data and data science
within the organization.

00:00:41.400 --> 00:00:45.410
Welcome, John, and thank
you for your partnership,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:49.640
as well as for being here today
to share Deutsche Bank's data

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:51.452
journey with us.

00:00:51.452 --> 00:00:53.160
JOHN ALLEN: Well,
thanks for inviting me.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:56.340
It's great to be
here with you today.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.100
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: Likewise.

00:00:58.100 --> 00:01:00.270
So let's get started.

00:01:00.270 --> 00:01:04.069
Let's take a look at the
enterprise data landscape,

00:01:04.069 --> 00:01:07.940
which as many of
you know is diverse

00:01:07.940 --> 00:01:11.690
and becoming increasingly
distributed with data that

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.710
is stored in data lakes, data
warehouses, specialized data

00:01:15.710 --> 00:01:21.020
marts, and databases, not
only on GCP but on prem,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.870
as well as in other clouds.

00:01:23.870 --> 00:01:25.730
Now, each one of
these systems have

00:01:25.730 --> 00:01:29.000
their own way of handling
metadata, security,

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.940
and governance.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:35.700
So on one hand, while the data
is becoming more distributed,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.800
the number of users within an
organization that need access,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:44.970
self-service access to this data
in the tools of their choice

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.070
is also growing.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:49.770
And as a result,
companies often find

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:53.130
it challenging to
consistently manage and govern

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:56.400
their data while making
this data universally

00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:00.660
discoverable and accessible
to the data consumers.

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:03.300
Honestly, it's an
operational nightmare

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.230
that often results in
data silos and impacts

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:12.910
the overall analytics agility
of most organizations.

00:02:12.910 --> 00:02:17.590
If you look at the data, 67%
of the data that is produced

00:02:17.590 --> 00:02:20.240
is actually never analyzed.

00:02:20.240 --> 00:02:23.720
And 68% of the companies
are unable to realize

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:28.380
any tangible, measurable
value from their data.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:32.880
What is even more
surprising is that only 16%

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:36.600
of the data consumers
that have access to data

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:40.490
actually trust the data
that they have access to.

00:02:40.490 --> 00:02:43.660
And this is exactly
why we built Dataplex--

00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:48.460
an intelligent data fabric that
unifies your distributed data

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:54.570
to help automate data management
and power analytics at scale.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:56.220
So what does that actually mean?

00:02:56.220 --> 00:03:00.200
So let's double-click
a little bit.

00:03:00.200 --> 00:03:05.720
Dataplex is built from ground up
for distributed data, requiring

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.980
you to not move your data
or duplicate your data.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:13.280
At the foundation of
Dataplex is its ability

00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:17.240
to logically represent data
and unify data from a metadata

00:03:17.240 --> 00:03:21.720
perspective regardless of
where this data is stored.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.680
We have focused on three
key areas of value--

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.280
intelligent, AI-driven
data management;

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:31.040
centralized security
and governance;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.650
and an integrated
analytics experience

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:39.250
bringing together both GCP
native and open-source tools.

00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:41.980
All of these areas
are tightly integrated

00:03:41.980 --> 00:03:45.580
in a task-centric
experience, helping

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.340
you build a flexible
data platform

00:03:48.340 --> 00:03:52.750
and making this data
accessible to your end users,

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:56.110
while ensuring that your
policies and best practices

00:03:56.110 --> 00:03:59.550
can be consistently enforced.

00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:01.650
Now, this is a
vision for Dataplex.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:06.420
We are starting out by unifying
the data that our customers use

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:09.780
for analytics on GCP
before extending it

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:11.970
to other sources outside of GCP.

00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:18.350
Taking a closer look at that
task-centric experience,

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.040
we've built an experience
that focuses on the operations

00:04:22.040 --> 00:04:26.540
that you need to undertake on
your data on a daily basis,

00:04:26.540 --> 00:04:29.720
starting from
curating and managing

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:35.060
that data to monitoring,
securing, analyzing this data.

00:04:35.060 --> 00:04:38.090
We have built key integrations
with existing products,

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:45.890
such as BigQuery, Dataproc,
Google Cloud Storage, Data

00:04:45.890 --> 00:04:49.160
Fusion, Dataflow, Data
Catalog, and others

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:52.490
to really bring together
that single pane of glass

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:56.480
that you can use to curate,
manage, monitor, secure,

00:04:56.480 --> 00:04:59.390
and analyze your data.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:02.990
With Dataplex, you can
really organize your data

00:05:02.990 --> 00:05:05.990
in a way that is meaningful
for your business

00:05:05.990 --> 00:05:09.200
around different domains based
on how this data is produced

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.110
and owned or how
this data is used,

00:05:12.110 --> 00:05:14.970
instead of where
this data is stored.

00:05:14.970 --> 00:05:17.180
You can define
standardized policies

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.140
around access control, data
quality, data classification,

00:05:21.140 --> 00:05:22.880
and lifecycle management.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:25.880
And you can leverage the
built-in data intelligence

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.120
and metadata propagation
capabilities of Dataplex

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:32.090
to get out-of-box
access to this data

00:05:32.090 --> 00:05:34.610
from a variety of
tools, both GCP

00:05:34.610 --> 00:05:38.290
native as well as open source.

00:05:38.290 --> 00:05:40.120
We have a number
of customers who

00:05:40.120 --> 00:05:43.540
have been our early
adopters using Dataplex.

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:47.260
And these customers come from
various different industry

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.110
verticals as well
as geographies.

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:54.190
And they're using Dataplex
as a foundational component

00:05:54.190 --> 00:05:57.130
of their data platform
and seeing value

00:05:57.130 --> 00:06:01.150
in having this unified data
fabric to manage and govern

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:03.130
their distributed data.

00:06:03.130 --> 00:06:06.790
And today, I'm really
happy to have John

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:12.460
here with us, who is going
to talk about how he sees

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:17.470
the value of data in the overall
strategy at Deutsche Bank,

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.090
his vision for the data
strategy, and the role

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.820
that Dataplex plays in
realizing that vision.

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:27.550
So welcome, John.

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:32.000
We are super excited
to have you here.

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:34.000
Could you tell us
a little bit more

00:06:34.000 --> 00:06:37.300
about yourself first and
then a little bit more

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.402
about Deutsche Bank and
how you use data today?

00:06:40.402 --> 00:06:41.110
JOHN ALLEN: Sure.

00:06:41.110 --> 00:06:42.010
Well, thanks again.

00:06:42.010 --> 00:06:43.040
It's great to be here.

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:47.290
So I work within Deutsche
Bank's chief data office,

00:06:47.290 --> 00:06:50.560
which is part of our Technology
and Data and Innovation

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.100
Division.

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:57.250
And our goal is really to enable
the secure supply, preparation,

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:01.070
and analysis of data across
the entire organization.

00:07:01.070 --> 00:07:03.520
Now in my role looking
at product strategy,

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.310
I spend a lot of my time
working with the business teams

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:09.040
and technology vendors
to ensure that we

00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:13.870
have the right roadmaps in place
to deliver those capabilities.

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:16.930
Deutsche Bank is the
leading German bank.

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:19.630
We have 84,000 employees
and operate just

00:07:19.630 --> 00:07:25.060
under 1,900 branches, with
offices in over 50 countries.

00:07:25.060 --> 00:07:28.750
The bank comprises of four
client-centric divisions--

00:07:28.750 --> 00:07:32.170
a corporate bank, a private
bank, an investment bank,

00:07:32.170 --> 00:07:35.500
and an asset manager, DWS,
which Deutsche Bank maintains

00:07:35.500 --> 00:07:37.600
a significant stake in.

00:07:37.600 --> 00:07:39.940
Now, being a global
bank, we rely

00:07:39.940 --> 00:07:44.710
upon a very complex global
technology estate comprising

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:47.050
of tens of thousands
of machines,

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:49.660
thousands of
applications, and hundreds

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:53.020
of operational and
analytical data stores.

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:57.310
Our total data footprint is
in excess of 100 petabytes.

00:07:57.310 --> 00:08:00.610
And we use that data for
a wonderfully diverse set

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.275
of use cases.

00:08:02.275 --> 00:08:03.460
I'm trying to think of some.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:04.570
In terms of our--

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:06.970
some of the more interesting
ones, our front office team

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.760
use data to drive
real-time analysis

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:12.580
and low latency
algorithmic trading,

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.340
and allows our product
managers to develop highly

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.650
personalized client offerings.

00:08:17.650 --> 00:08:19.420
In the middle and
back office, we

00:08:19.420 --> 00:08:22.330
leverage big data processing
to help manage risk

00:08:22.330 --> 00:08:24.220
and to prevent fraud.

00:08:24.220 --> 00:08:28.810
And like any business, we rely
upon highly accurate and timely

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:31.990
financial data to serve
regulatory and financial

00:08:31.990 --> 00:08:33.280
reporting requirements.

00:08:37.246 --> 00:08:39.909
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: You operate
in such a global market.

00:08:39.909 --> 00:08:42.490
And the complexities of
operations that you just

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:45.850
mentioned across different
businesses within the bank,

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.170
it's quite interesting.

00:08:48.170 --> 00:08:50.410
What role does data
play at Deutsche Bank?

00:08:50.410 --> 00:08:55.180
And what has your journey
with data been like so far?

00:08:55.180 --> 00:08:56.300
JOHN ALLEN: Well, I agree.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:08:59.140
I mean, fundamentally
we're a data business.

00:08:59.140 --> 00:09:02.110
And data is the
digital manifestation

00:09:02.110 --> 00:09:05.890
of our clients, their
transactions, relationships,

00:09:05.890 --> 00:09:08.270
and our internal processes.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:12.010
However, we didn't always view
ourselves as a data business.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:15.310
Instead, we had a more
application-centric mindset,

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:18.430
which saw data is more of a
side effect of that application

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:19.240
processing.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.270
We locked data away
in disparate silos

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:25.300
with inconsistent
taxonomies and formats,

00:09:25.300 --> 00:09:29.050
and a lack of clear ownership
and an over-reliance

00:09:29.050 --> 00:09:32.210
on bilateral
point-to-point data feeds.

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:35.020
So to address this, we
established a long-term plan,

00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:38.320
our data strategy, to
improve the availability

00:09:38.320 --> 00:09:41.980
of high quality data organized
around a set of consistent data

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:46.240
models and golden sources,
to uplift skills in data

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:48.250
and increase understanding
of data problems

00:09:48.250 --> 00:09:50.440
across the entire organization.

00:09:50.440 --> 00:09:54.070
And we set out to enable modern
analytics, such as big data

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.660
and machine learning.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:58.510
The transformation took
sustained investments

00:09:58.510 --> 00:10:02.680
over many years and was
executed in an environment

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:05.380
of ever-changing regulations.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:07.060
Just as an aside,
last year, there

00:10:07.060 --> 00:10:10.180
were over 6,000
pages of new rules

00:10:10.180 --> 00:10:13.450
that we needed to examine
to understand their impact

00:10:13.450 --> 00:10:15.820
on that data landscape.

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:19.720
Today, we are a much more
data-centric organization,

00:10:19.720 --> 00:10:21.580
which actively
manages and improves

00:10:21.580 --> 00:10:25.480
its data as part of the
normal day-to-day operations.

00:10:25.480 --> 00:10:27.790
We operate an
integrated ecosystem

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:29.390
of analytics platforms.

00:10:29.390 --> 00:10:31.240
And we have the
key trusted sources

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:34.210
of high quality business
data needed to drive

00:10:34.210 --> 00:10:36.550
intelligent decision making.

00:10:36.550 --> 00:10:37.863
I know you asked for successes.

00:10:37.863 --> 00:10:40.030
And I think, frankly, there
are too many to mention.

00:10:40.030 --> 00:10:42.130
But from my personal
perspective,

00:10:42.130 --> 00:10:44.200
I think the work
the private bank has

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.570
done creating new
personalized offerings

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.630
for our retail clients through
the application of machine

00:10:49.630 --> 00:10:53.170
learning is especially
cool, as is the big data

00:10:53.170 --> 00:10:57.700
processing that our risk teams
use to allow us to be much more

00:10:57.700 --> 00:10:59.647
efficient with our capital.

00:10:59.647 --> 00:11:01.230
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: It's
quite interesting

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.860
to hear how you have
made this transition,

00:11:04.860 --> 00:11:06.360
or the bank has
made this transition

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:10.020
from an application-centric
view to a data-centric view

00:11:10.020 --> 00:11:13.800
in an industry that is so highly
regulated with, as you just

00:11:13.800 --> 00:11:18.550
mentioned, changing regulations
and compliance rules.

00:11:18.550 --> 00:11:21.100
So what have been your top
learnings and challenges

00:11:21.100 --> 00:11:23.240
so far?

00:11:23.240 --> 00:11:26.400
JOHN ALLEN: Well, for me some of
the most significant learnings

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.050
relate to governance, ownership,
and how we as an organization

00:11:31.050 --> 00:11:32.280
think about data--

00:11:32.280 --> 00:11:35.460
our data culture, if you will.

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.220
Foremost for me, data needs
to be treated as a first class

00:11:38.220 --> 00:11:42.480
citizen within the enterprise
if it is to be used and reused

00:11:42.480 --> 00:11:43.500
effectively.

00:11:43.500 --> 00:11:46.950
It must be seen as an
asset in its own right.

00:11:46.950 --> 00:11:49.800
Also, finding the right
balance between security

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.170
and stability,
coupled with agility

00:11:52.170 --> 00:11:56.990
to deliver analytics and
help customers, is difficult.

00:11:56.990 --> 00:12:00.890
Maintaining distinctly separate
operational and analytical

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.820
organizations fails to
deliver analytical stability

00:12:04.820 --> 00:12:06.380
and efficiency.

00:12:06.380 --> 00:12:09.680
What I mean there is this
is when those teams do not

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:14.900
use and thus value each
other's representations of what

00:12:14.900 --> 00:12:18.920
is, in essence, the same data.

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:23.300
Metadata is also a critical
foundation for effective data

00:12:23.300 --> 00:12:25.440
management and data governance.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:27.140
However, building
and maintaining

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:30.920
a unified repository of
high quality metadata

00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.970
is a significant challenge in
traditional IT environments.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:39.350
And finally, I think while
centralization of data

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:42.140
can seem like an attractive
approach to achieving

00:12:42.140 --> 00:12:45.740
consistency and standards and
the application of policies,

00:12:45.740 --> 00:12:48.650
it very often leads
to bottlenecks

00:12:48.650 --> 00:12:51.793
that inhibit business agility.

00:12:51.793 --> 00:12:53.960
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: So given
these learnings, especially

00:12:53.960 --> 00:12:56.360
learnings around how to
build trust in data, how

00:12:56.360 --> 00:12:59.180
to avoid data duplication,
how to put metadata

00:12:59.180 --> 00:13:03.500
at the center and foundation
of your data platform,

00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:08.090
could you share with us what is
your short-term and long-term

00:13:08.090 --> 00:13:10.790
data strategy for the bank?

00:13:10.790 --> 00:13:12.060
JOHN ALLEN: Yeah, I'd love to.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:16.610
Well, our goal is to democratize
the use of data and analytics

00:13:16.610 --> 00:13:19.820
by making it a secure
and frictionless

00:13:19.820 --> 00:13:21.950
self-service experience.

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.650
Only then will we
be able to make

00:13:24.650 --> 00:13:26.900
data accessible to
everyone and not

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.930
just the data experts
within the organization.

00:13:29.930 --> 00:13:31.640
We intend to do
this by investing

00:13:31.640 --> 00:13:34.160
in the automation of
controls and policies,

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:38.390
which along with high quality
metadata, technical, business,

00:13:38.390 --> 00:13:42.380
and contextual metadata,
will allow us to build a data

00:13:42.380 --> 00:13:44.990
control plane that
ensures that our data is

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:48.710
easy to find and access,
yet always remains secure

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.900
wherever it is used.

00:13:50.900 --> 00:13:53.480
Now, currently we're
completing our transition

00:13:53.480 --> 00:13:58.160
to managing data as an asset,
changing our IT processes

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:01.790
to allow data to thrive outside
of those owning applications

00:14:01.790 --> 00:14:03.470
we traditionally had.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.690
We're also converging
on common approaches

00:14:05.690 --> 00:14:07.970
to data sharing and
data re-use, which

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:11.360
use streamlined data
governance processes,

00:14:11.360 --> 00:14:13.700
a new unified
repository of metadata,

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:17.610
and automation to make
our data more accessible.

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:19.140
To help our engineering
teams, we're

00:14:19.140 --> 00:14:22.200
also developing standard
reference architectures

00:14:22.200 --> 00:14:24.870
for common data problems,
such as streaming and batch

00:14:24.870 --> 00:14:28.440
analytics, and machine
learning in GCP.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:30.480
And finally, we're
investing in our people

00:14:30.480 --> 00:14:35.220
with training in Cloud and
agile data best practices.

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.550
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: So speaking
of cloud and your partnership

00:14:38.550 --> 00:14:42.810
with Google Cloud, how
has Google Cloud helped

00:14:42.810 --> 00:14:46.035
you realize your data vision?

00:14:46.035 --> 00:14:47.910
JOHN ALLEN: Well, the
partnership with Google

00:14:47.910 --> 00:14:51.420
has given us a fantastic base
to build our future banking

00:14:51.420 --> 00:14:53.100
services on.

00:14:53.100 --> 00:14:57.450
Technologies such as GCS,
BigQuery, Dataflow, Dataproc

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:00.840
are much more powerful
and easy to use than our

00:15:00.840 --> 00:15:02.730
on premise equivalents.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:07.110
Likewise, Cloud SQL, Datastore,
GCE, App Engine, and Pub/Sub

00:15:07.110 --> 00:15:09.280
for transactional systems.

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:11.100
However, for me
what's really exciting

00:15:11.100 --> 00:15:14.250
is the advancements in machine
learning that Vertex AI

00:15:14.250 --> 00:15:17.070
and BigQuery ML can bring.

00:15:17.070 --> 00:15:19.440
But that's not to say
it's all plain sailing.

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.170
There is still a lot of work
to integrate and assemble

00:15:22.170 --> 00:15:25.410
these products into
effective business solutions.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.760
And this is especially true in
a risk averse, highly regulated

00:15:29.760 --> 00:15:31.410
industry such as ours.

00:15:31.410 --> 00:15:35.520
Speaking frankly, there is
still quite a gap to close.

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:38.280
I think this is an area where
we see the partnership being

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:40.330
especially beneficial.

00:15:40.330 --> 00:15:42.630
So Google understands
this complexity.

00:15:42.630 --> 00:15:44.700
And together we are
developing approaches

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:47.490
to solve these
problems in the Cloud.

00:15:47.490 --> 00:15:49.980
In fact, we are already
seeing some exciting product

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:51.540
developments from Google.

00:15:51.540 --> 00:15:55.520
A good example of that
is indeed Dataplex.

00:15:55.520 --> 00:15:57.710
PRAJAKTA DAMLE:
Your team has been

00:15:57.710 --> 00:15:59.660
an early adopter of Dataplex.

00:15:59.660 --> 00:16:02.960
And your close partnership
is extremely valuable for us

00:16:02.960 --> 00:16:07.790
as you provide us guidance
on product direction.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:09.950
I would love to
hear more about how

00:16:09.950 --> 00:16:13.820
you see Dataplex fit in
your overall data strategy,

00:16:13.820 --> 00:16:17.330
how Dataplex is helping you
with some of the data challenges

00:16:17.330 --> 00:16:19.122
that you mentioned early on.

00:16:19.122 --> 00:16:20.330
JOHN ALLEN: Yeah, absolutely.

00:16:20.330 --> 00:16:23.090
Well, we are looking to
use Dataplex to give us

00:16:23.090 --> 00:16:27.320
a way of organizing our data
around our core business

00:16:27.320 --> 00:16:30.140
and data concepts,
such as global markets

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:32.270
data, payments
data, customer data,

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:36.230
without having to copy that data
from its originating mastering

00:16:36.230 --> 00:16:37.970
application projects.

00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:41.750
Dataplex's lakes and zones
give us an elegant way to group

00:16:41.750 --> 00:16:46.070
our data assets depending upon
their type, their quality,

00:16:46.070 --> 00:16:49.010
or their stage in
their data lifecycle.

00:16:49.010 --> 00:16:50.810
And we're experimenting
with the designs

00:16:50.810 --> 00:16:53.270
for operating this at
an enterprise level.

00:16:53.270 --> 00:16:56.580
These zones are very
important concept for us.

00:16:56.580 --> 00:16:59.720
In fact, we implemented
something similar in our

00:16:59.720 --> 00:17:01.370
on premise data lake.

00:17:01.370 --> 00:17:05.089
However with Dataplex, we can
take this to the next level,

00:17:05.089 --> 00:17:08.180
realizing a much more
federated system,

00:17:08.180 --> 00:17:10.950
employing a type of data mesh
architecture, if you will,

00:17:10.950 --> 00:17:14.150
where data remains in
situ, owned and governed

00:17:14.150 --> 00:17:16.220
by its originators,
yet still part

00:17:16.220 --> 00:17:19.430
of a unified platform
maintained by Dataplex

00:17:19.430 --> 00:17:23.695
and integrated with a set
of leading analytical tools.

00:17:23.695 --> 00:17:28.550
I think GCP's Data Catalog also
gives us amazing capabilities.

00:17:28.550 --> 00:17:32.000
It gives us visibility of key
organizational data assets.

00:17:32.000 --> 00:17:35.150
But with Dataplex, we
can go a step further

00:17:35.150 --> 00:17:38.330
and extend the Data Catalog
with the automated discovery

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:40.880
and classification of
semi-structured and

00:17:40.880 --> 00:17:43.330
unstructured data.

00:17:43.330 --> 00:17:47.620
Now ensuring our data is secure
is of paramount importance.

00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:50.370
And we're investing heavily
in the automation of controls

00:17:50.370 --> 00:17:54.240
and embedding those controls
into our infrastructure as code

00:17:54.240 --> 00:17:55.870
and as policies.

00:17:55.870 --> 00:17:58.410
However, today we still need
to develop those controls

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:00.510
for each data service we use.

00:18:00.510 --> 00:18:03.120
We also need to find a
way of enforcing them

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.010
through infrastructure
as code templates

00:18:05.010 --> 00:18:06.750
and tools such as Terraform.

00:18:06.750 --> 00:18:08.820
This adds complexity.

00:18:08.820 --> 00:18:11.310
With Dataplex, we
have the potential

00:18:11.310 --> 00:18:15.990
to develop these controls once
for the lake, zone, and assets,

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:19.410
and let Dataplex do the
hard work of applying them

00:18:19.410 --> 00:18:22.200
to the different
underlying technologies.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.440
And the plans Dataplex has for
extending this policy driven

00:18:25.440 --> 00:18:27.930
approach to include
data governance, data

00:18:27.930 --> 00:18:32.190
management, and data quality
tasks is particularly exciting.

00:18:32.190 --> 00:18:36.630
We can imagine a world where we
assess, transform, and promote

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:39.660
our high quality data
based upon policies

00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:42.930
and not hand coded data
pipelines, allowing

00:18:42.930 --> 00:18:45.750
us to focus more on the
value creating tasks

00:18:45.750 --> 00:18:49.750
and less on maintaining
boilerplate data integration.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:53.190
So in summary, I think we
see BigQuery for analytics

00:18:53.190 --> 00:18:58.230
and Dataplex for data management
as cornerstones of our vision

00:18:58.230 --> 00:19:01.050
for enabling a secure
and frictionless data

00:19:01.050 --> 00:19:03.317
experience in the cloud.

00:19:03.317 --> 00:19:04.900
PRAJAKTA DAMLE: Well,
thank you, John.

00:19:04.900 --> 00:19:07.270
Thank you for sharing your
insights and perspective

00:19:07.270 --> 00:19:07.973
with us today.

00:19:07.973 --> 00:19:09.015
JOHN ALLEN: Your welcome.

00:19:09.015 --> 00:19:10.780
PRAJAKTA DAMLE:
And also, thank you

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:14.840
to you and your team for
your continued partnership.

00:19:14.840 --> 00:19:18.860
So as you heard
John just outline,

00:19:18.860 --> 00:19:24.680
Dataplex helps enterprises
unlock the freedom of choice

00:19:24.680 --> 00:19:26.450
by providing them
the flexibility

00:19:26.450 --> 00:19:30.230
to keep data where they see fit
and to use the tools that they

00:19:30.230 --> 00:19:35.330
need to run analysis
within their organization.

00:19:35.330 --> 00:19:38.690
Dataplex provides a way to
enable consistent controls

00:19:38.690 --> 00:19:43.580
across enterprise data landscape
that is inherently distributed.

00:19:43.580 --> 00:19:46.670
And finally, with
Dataplex our customers

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:49.730
can leverage the built-in
data intelligence

00:19:49.730 --> 00:19:54.050
to automate many of
the common data tasks

00:19:54.050 --> 00:19:56.330
around data management
and governance

00:19:56.330 --> 00:19:59.460
across their data estate.

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:02.640
So that's all that
we have today.

00:20:02.640 --> 00:20:04.850
If you would like to
learn more about Dataplex,

00:20:04.850 --> 00:20:06.800
do check our website.

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:09.620
And thank you, everyone,
for your time today

00:20:09.620 --> 00:20:13.730
and for attending
this session on how

00:20:13.730 --> 00:20:17.960
Dataplex helps enterprises
break down their data silos.

00:20:17.960 --> 00:20:19.160
Thanks again.

00:20:19.160 --> 00:20:20.710
JOHN ALLEN: Thanks.

